So do all Crawl wins.  Hell, so does managing to get as far as
collecting a rune in Crawl.

There's degrees of impressiveness in a Crawl victory.  The first is
to manage the simplest possible victory, by collecting the serpentine,
decaying, and silver runes from the Snake Pit, the Swamp, and the
Vaults, then going into the Realm of Zot and retrieving the Orb of Zot
from the insanely dangerous denizens.  It is quite unremarkable for
people to die horribly on Zot:5 the first couple of times they get a
character that far.

From there... well, there's a bunch of other well-differentiated
ways through the game.  Won with a deep elf conjurer worshipping
Vehumet (the ultimate ranged-attack glass cannon)?  Great.  Now go back
with a minotaur berserker worshipping Trog the Angry (a strong, sturdy,
capable fighter with the added bonus of being able to berserk, at the
expense of not being allowed to learn or cast spells), or a sludge elf
transmuter worshipping Warmaster Okawaru (a fragile but devastating
engine of melee-range destruction), or perhaps something less obvious.

Feeling like something weird?  Play a worshipper of the divine gambler
Nemelex Xobeh, or the capricious chaos-god Xom.

Feeling daring?  Take on the Hells, or the Tomb, or Pandemonium.  It's
often reckoned that these places are harder than Zot:1-4, and possibly
even harder than Zot:5.

The most magnificent feat, if a little out of character for Crawl (since
it involves grinding Pandemonium and the Abyss for runes), is an
all-runes victory, where you collect all the possible runes before
entering the Realm of Zot.

